course: everyday
lesson: 438
title: Funny pranks
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Ollie in Bristol put a rubber spider in his dad's slipper. Dad screamed like a kid, then laughed so hard he cried. «Proper job, son!» — West Country for «well done». The next week Dad got him back with a rubber spider in the cereal. A prank is a trick played for fun, and a good one has three marks: nobody is hurt, nothing is broken, and everyone laughs at the end — including the one who was pranked.
explain: The best pranks are silly surprises. Sophie put googly eyes on everything in the fridge, and when Mum opened it a dozen eyes stared at her; Mum laughed and the photo went on the family group chat. Jayden in Atlanta wrapped his brother's whole desk in tin foil, pencil by pencil, which took two hours; his brother walked in and said «Bro, that's actually bussin'» — young slang for «amazing». The bigger the effort and the smaller the harm, the better the prank.
explain: Some pranks go a bit wrong. Mia swapped salt for sugar in her brother's tea; he spat it across the table, laughed, and Mum made Mia make a fresh cup and clean up — fair enough. Leo jumped out at his sister from behind a door; Gran said «not near the stairs», a good point. Theo saw a cling-film-on-the-toilet prank online and tried it; it went everywhere, and Mum made him clean the whole bathroom. «Not so funny now, eh?» If you'd hate cleaning it up, it isn't a good prank.
explain: Some pranks cost people things they need. Ava set the clocks five minutes fast; Dad was early all week, but Gran nearly missed her bus home. Ruby in Edinburgh said the spelling test was cancelled, then forgot to say «only joking»; her friend didn't study, got a low mark and was raging, and Ruby said sorry and helped her practise for the retest. Kirsty in Aberdeen hid her dad's car keys so he'd «look for ages» — he was late and nearly missed a job interview. «That's why we don't hide keys, pet. You never know what's riding on them.»
explain: Noah's family in Vancouver has a «prank week» every summer with three rules: nothing that breaks, nothing that hurts, nothing that's mean. Everyone looks forward to it, nobody gets upset, and Grandpa wins every year with a rubber snake in the mailbox.
fact: A prank is a trick played for fun, not to hurt.
fact: A good prank causes no harm and no damage, and the person pranked laughs too.
fact: Pranks that need cleaning up should be cleaned up by the prankster.
fact: Food pranks are funny once, but never with anything that could make someone ill or with someone's allergy.
fact: Jump scares should never be done near stairs, hot drinks or someone who could be hurt by a fright.
fact: Pranks that mess with time, travel, money or tests can cost people things that matter.
fact: A pranked person needs to be told «only joking» before the trick causes harm.
fact: Never prank with things people need for work, safety or health, like keys or medicine.
fact: Pranks that everyone agrees to play, with clear rules, are the best kind.
fact: If you would hate the clean-up or hate it done to you, it is not a good prank.
fact: «Proper job» (West Country) means well done; «bussin'» (young US slang) means amazing; «raging» (UK, Ireland) means very angry.
exercise: What did Ollie put in his dad's slipper? | A rubber spider | A real frog | A wet sponge | A bag of flour
exercise: What did Dad say after the spider prank? | «Proper job, son!» | «Never do that again!» | «Go to your room, now!» | «I knew it was there»
exercise: How did Ollie's dad get him back? | A rubber spider in the cereal | Hiding his football in the shed | Salt in his tea | A fake test from school
exercise: What happened after Mia's salt-in-the-tea prank? | She made a fresh cup and cleaned the table | She was grounded for a whole month | Her brother drank the whole cup and was sick all night | Nothing at all
exercise: Where did Gran say jump scares should never happen? | Near the stairs | In the garden | In the kitchen | At the park
exercise: What did Sophie stick on the fridge food? | Googly eyes | Fake plastic spiders | Funny labels | Coloured stickers
exercise: What did Jayden wrap in tin foil? | His brother's whole desk | The family car in the drive | The fridge | His own bed and pillow
exercise: Why was Ava's clock prank not completely harmless? | Gran nearly missed her bus home | Dad was late for an important meeting | The clocks broke | Mum missed the dentist
exercise: What went wrong with Ruby's «test cancelled» joke? | She forgot to say she was joking, and her friend didn't study | The test really was cancelled, so nobody believed her again | The teacher laughed and cancelled it | Her friend got full marks
exercise: How did Ruby put it right? | She said sorry and helped her friend practise | She asked the teacher to let her friend sit the test again next week | She told the teacher it was a lie | She did nothing
exercise: What did Theo have to do after the cling-film prank? | Clean the whole bathroom | Buy a new roll of cling film | Write a sorry note to his mum | Nothing, because it was funny
exercise: Why was hiding Dad's car keys a bad prank? | He was late and nearly missed a job interview | Keys are very expensive to replace, and the car needed new locks | Dad never drives | The keys got lost forever behind the sofa
exercise: What are the three rules of Noah's family prank week? | Nothing that breaks, hurts or is mean | Only at night, only on Grandpa, only with snakes | No food, no clocks, no keys | Anything goes for a week
exercise: What is Grandpa's winning prank every year? | A rubber snake in the mailbox | Googly eyes on all the food in the fridge | Salt in everyone's tea at breakfast | Hiding the car keys every morning
exercise: What makes a prank a good one? | No harm, no damage, and everyone laughs | Someone gets really scared and screams the house down | It costs a lot of money | Only the prankster laughs at the end
test: Brother sees his desk in tin foil: «Bro, that's actually bussin'». How does he feel? | He thinks it's amazing | He's furious | He's scared of it | He's bored
test: Mate: «saw this cling film toilet prank on YouTube, gonna do it». What's the honest warning? | You'll be the one cleaning it up | It never works in real life, only on video | Toilets at home don't fit cling film | It's always hilarious for everyone
test: Ruby's friend got a low mark and was raging. What does «raging» mean? | Very angry | Very happy | Very tired | Very hungry
test: What would have happened if Ruby had said «only joking!» a minute later? | Her friend would have studied and been fine | The test would have been cancelled for real | Her friend would still fail | The teacher would punish Ruby
test: Kirsty: «I only hid his keys for fun». Dad: «You never know what's riding on them.» What does he mean? | Keys may be needed for something important | Keys are needed for riding a bike to work | Kids shouldn't touch cars | He doesn't like jokes about keys
test: Kid wants to prank his sister with salt in her drink — she has a bad tummy today. Good idea? | No, food pranks must never risk making someone ill | Yes, salt is always harmless | Yes, as long as she laughs about it later on when she feels better | Only if it's a lot of salt
test: Leo wants to jump out at Gran on the stairs. Why not? | A fright on the stairs could make her fall | Gran doesn't like any kind of jokes or tricks at all | Stairs are boring | It's too easy to be funny
test: Ava set all the clocks forward. Which is the smartest add-on? | Tell people before it makes them miss something | Set them an hour forward next time | Never tell anyone, it's funnier that way | Change the phones too
test: In a prank video someone gets soaked and furious, and the prankster laughs alone. Is it a good prank? | No, the pranked person must laugh too | Yes, any big reaction is a good one for a video | Yes, if it gets lots of views | No, but only because of the water
test: Family prank week rules: nothing breaks, nothing hurts, nothing mean. Which prank fits? | Googly eyes on the fridge food | Hiding Mum's medicine | Breaking a cup and blaming the dog | Making fun of someone's looks
test: Ollie's dad: «Proper job, son!» How does he feel about the spider? | He thinks it was a great prank | He's angry about the spider | He wants Ollie to tidy up the slippers | He didn't notice the spider at all
exam: Identify the characteristics which, taken together, distinguish a good-natured prank. | That no person is harmed, nothing is damaged, and the subject also laughs | That the subject is frightened for an extended period | That property is damaged for amusement | That only the perpetrator derives amusement from it
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the concealment of a parent's car keys by way of jest. | It may cause the parent to be late for engagements of importance | It is invariably harmless, keys being readily found in the household | It is the most suitable form of prank | It improves the punctuality of the parent
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a false announcement that a test has been cancelled, not subsequently withdrawn? | It may cause another pupil to forgo preparation and obtain a low mark | It is harmless, being a mere jest | It assists the other pupil to rest | It obliges the teacher to cancel the test
exam: It is given that a family prohibits, during an annual prank week, any prank which breaks, hurts or is unkind. Determine the consequence reported. | All participants anticipate the week and none is distressed | All participants are distressed | No pranks whatever are performed during the week, the rules forbidding all of them | The rules are disregarded by the grandfather
